  fancy modeling or no fancy stats really  going on here this is an example from  the United Kingdom's National Health  Service they have a single-payer system  in there everyone can go get free  treatment from the National Health  Service and they actually use data to  set expectations for the patient and for  the provider about what treatment  success looks like about what your  symptoms are likely to look like as you  go into treatment and as you continue  treatment in this case what they do is  they go and get all of the people that  they've treated before in these large  registries they have millions of  patients that they've treated over the  last you know five or ten years or so  and then they they use packages like LME  for and NL me4 to fit growth curve  models but really the idea here is that  they take all of the people they've ever  seen before and then they just plot that  back to the clinician but giving them  some guardrails so we say of all of the  people that we've treated before this is  what the worst ten percent of outcomes  look like and this is what the best ten  percent of outcomes look like and then  all they do is show you what this  patient looks like on their own you know  unique journey and then they'll give you  an alert if you ever go outside of those  two so you know in this case you can see  this patient starts off and it's going  okay but then you know something happens  and  they fall into this this red zone that  has shown back to the clinicians and  then the clinician is told hey you know  this looks like whatever's going on here  this looks abnormally bad for us you  know this is already in the the worst  ten percent of outcomes given everything  that we know from all of the people that  we treat and then they give that they  give that information to the care team  and so that the care team can have a  conversation amongst themself and say  hey do we know what's going on here is  there some kind of external reason why  this might have happened or do we need  to have a conversation with that patient  and let them know hey you know what  we're doing right now isn't working we'd  love to have a conversation about  different treatment strategies that we  could take you know we really want to  help get you back on track this is a  super simple innovation from a data  science perspective right there's really  not that much going on on the modeling  side but the impact can be pretty  profound so a randomized control trial  of this approach this framework the  results of which is shown on the right  so if you randomized people to see the  same sets of providers in two different  cases but in one case they given this  kind of visualization and they're given  these heads up on patients that are not  recovering and on the other side they're  treated by the same people but without  this technology supporting the  clinicians you actually see that failure  rates for treatment are actually two  times lower if they were given this  heads-up about you know someone's  progress not being on track so a great  example of data science done at scale  used every single day in the National  Health Service that really didn't depend  on on that sophisticated modeling but  does really speak to a pretty  fundamental understanding of what's  going on with that patient and how we  can try and give clinicians information  that is informative for them and and  helpful also back to the patient the  final example you know by this point the  the patient's of hopefully all recovered  but even after they get better many of  those people will eventually relapse  it's you know an unfortunate truth that  many mental health conditions are  actually recurring and people will you  know often dip in and out of good at  versus bad periods over the course of  their life but the the individual  journey that one person goes through is  quite noisy right if you take this  figure on the left this is what it might  look like if you have you know 200  people and they're all going on their  own you  journeys and that can actually be pretty  challenging either for a clinician to  predict upfront offer a machine learning  model to also try and predict what that  individual pathway might look like and  so this was a study done by a biostats  group they used the L CMM package to fit  late latent class mixture models which  basically say out of all of the  different individual journeys that are  going on can we come up with more  representative classes like types of  response that people generally go on  turns out you can you look on the right  and you can actually see these somewhat  more interpretable outcomes and then you  can actually try and predict those  instead so a clinician instead of having  to worry about the specific nuances of  what that person's journey might look  like would instead just get a heads-up  saying this is someone who looks like  they're gonna maybe follow a trajectory  that looks generally bad versus this  looks like someone who's going to be on  a trajectory it looks fairly okay and  then again that can be used to allocate  resources to the health system it can be  used to motivate the the individual  patient and help them stay better once  they are better so I hope that this talk  has has convinced you I think there are  is that mental health isn't just a huge  field of opportunity for people who have  an r background or a data science  background that want to make a  difference individual patients I think  that in I've shown a bunch of cases  where new technology particularly  implemented through our stats can  improve mental healthcare right now not  only can they help but there's actually  a ton of different places where we can  do a much better job it's not just one  specific area that's kind of hyper  developed it really is a green field  where people can help with many  different problems and it's not just  research and development we can do this  in the real world with real patients  we're doing it right now with tens of  thousands of people being treated every  day you know large health systems are  doing this kind of work there's really  tremendous opportunity for people do  this everywhere and my friends from grad  school made me put this in just because  the first time I came here I was an  attendee and I was in grad school and  you know there's anyone who's in grad  school knows that mental health issues  completely rampant and so I did have to  include some unofficial conclusions the  first one of which is that impostor  syndrome is extremely real but it's  important for everyone to remember that  great data science comes from very  diverse backgrounds right it doesn't  really matter what your experience is  really all you have to do is pick a  problem that you care about and then  pursue it kind of relentlessly and have  the the willingness to learn whatever  you need to learn to do it installed up  packages tidy verse and carrot really  can't change your life it took my life  in a very direct different direction but  between those two packages alone you can  actually have a huge impact on the way  that healthcare is delivered there's an  extreme amount of power just in two  simple are packages that help you do a  you know a tremendous amount with the  code that you have and I probably owe my  own sanity in mental health to Max Kuhn  and his colleagues for the developing  carrot I said this term last night it  was very embarrassing over dinner but  for sure I would have gone crazy if I  had to learn machine learning without  you know infrastructure and support that  helps you be able to do this do  reproducibly do it quickly do it  effectively  yeah there's everything we are hiring if  anyone is interested in you know picking  a problem in mental health and then  using data to try and make it better  definitely give us a shout you can email  me careers at Spring House  [Applause]
 

